    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`FULLTEXT` indexes are used with `MATCH() AGAINST()` for natural-language searches on text columns. They are available for `MyISAM` and `InnoDB` (since 5.6).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `FULLTEXT` index accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `FULLTEXT` indexes are used with `MATCH() AGAINST()` for natural-language searches on text columns.\n2. They are available for `MyISAM` and `InnoDB` (since 5.6).\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `FULLTEXT` indexes are used with `MATCH() AGAINST()` for natural-language searches on text columns.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
