    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTable-level locks (MyISAM) lock the entire table, reducing concurrency. Row-level locks (InnoDB) lock only specific rows, allowing higher concurrency but with more overhead.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between table-level and row-level locking and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Table-level locks (MyISAM) lock the entire table, reducing concurrency.\n2. Row-level locks (InnoDB) lock only specific rows, allowing higher concurrency but with more overhead.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Table-level locks (MyISAM) lock the entire table, reducing concurrency.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Table-level locks (MyISAM) lock the entire table, reducing concurrency.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
