    slug: "trigger-rollback",
    shortDescription: "Yes, if the trigger is in a transaction, you can rollback to undo the triggering statement (InnoDB).",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nYes, you can use `ROLLBACK` inside a trigger if the trigger is executed within a transaction. For InnoDB tables, the triggering DML statement is part of a transaction, so you can roll back the entire transaction including the trigger's actions. However, be careful: rolling back in a trigger can be disruptive; use it only for critical validation failures. Also, `ROLLBACK` in a trigger will abort the entire transaction, not just the trigger.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question is useful because yes, if the trigger is in a transaction, you can rollback to undo the triggering statement (InnoDB). The interviewer can then probe the mechanism, limitation, or practical consequence behind that answer.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Yes, you can use `ROLLBACK` inside a trigger if the trigger is executed within a transaction.\n2. For InnoDB tables, the triggering DML statement is part of a transaction, so you can roll back the entire transaction including the trigger's actions.\n3. However, be careful: rolling back in a trigger can be disruptive; use it only for critical validation failures.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Yes, you can use `ROLLBACK` inside a trigger if the trigger is executed within a transaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, can you use `rollback` inside a trigger matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
