    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLogical backup (mysqldump) exports SQL statements, is portable, but slower for large databases. Physical backup (copying data files) is faster but less portable and requires the server to be stopped.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between logical and physical backup and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Logical backup (mysqldump) exports SQL statements, is portable, but slower for large databases.\n2. Physical backup (copying data files) is faster but less portable and requires the server to be stopped.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Logical backup (mysqldump) exports SQL statements, is portable, but slower for large databases.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Logical backup (mysqldump) exports SQL statements, is portable, but slower for large databases.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
