    slug: "single-transaction",
    shortDescription: "Explain its importance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt ensures a consistent snapshot of InnoDB tables by starting a transaction and using MVCC. It avoids locking tables and is essential for non-blocking backups.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `--single-transaction` option in mysqldump accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. It ensures a consistent snapshot of InnoDB tables by starting a transaction and using MVCC.\n2. It avoids locking tables and is essential for non-blocking backups.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nPerform the debit and credit inside one transaction and commit only after both statements succeed; on failure, roll back the transaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. It ensures a consistent snapshot of InnoDB tables by starting a transaction and using MVCC.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
