    question: "What are the privileges needed to use `LOAD DATA INFILE`?",
    slug: "load-data-privileges",
    shortDescription: "Requires `FILE` privilege or `SUPER` for local files.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTo use `LOAD DATA INFILE` to read files from the server, you need the `FILE` privilege (global). For loading local files (`LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE`), you need the `FILE` privilege and also the `local_infile` system variable must be enabled. Additionally, the `--secure-file-priv` variable may restrict which directories can be read. For security, avoid granting `FILE` unless absolutely necessary, and consider using `LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE` with proper user permissions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. To use `LOAD DATA INFILE` to read files from the server, you need the `FILE` privilege (global).\n2. For loading local files (`LOAD DATA LOCAL INFILE`), you need the `FILE` privilege and also the `local_infile` system variable must be enabled.\n3. Additionally, the `--secure-file-priv` variable may restrict which directories can be read.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. To use `LOAD DATA INFILE` to read files from the server, you need the `FILE` privilege (global).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the privileges needed to use `load data infile` matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
