    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMemcached is a simple, distributed in-memory cache with a key-value store. Redis is more feature-rich, supporting persistence, complex data types (lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes), replication, and transactions. Redis is often used as a cache and a lightweight database.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between redis and memcached and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Memcached is a simple, distributed in-memory cache with a key-value store.\n2. Redis is more feature-rich, supporting persistence, complex data types (lists, sets, sorted sets, hashes), replication, and transactions.\n3. Redis is often used as a cache and a lightweight database.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Memcached is a simple, distributed in-memory cache with a key-value store.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Memcached is a simple, distributed in-memory cache with a key-value store.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
