    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAt most once: messages may be lost but never delivered twice. At least once: messages are delivered at least once, possibly duplicating. Exactly once: each message is delivered exactly once. Common in message queues.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the concept of 'at least once', 'at most once', and 'exactly once' delivery semantics. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. At most once: messages may be lost but never delivered twice.\n2. At least once: messages are delivered at least once, possibly duplicating.\n3. Exactly once: each message is delivered exactly once.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. At most once: messages may be lost but never delivered twice.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. At most once: messages may be lost but never delivered twice.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
