    question: "What are microservices? What are the pros and cons?",
    slug: "microservices-pros-cons",
    shortDescription: "Discuss the architecture.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMicroservices are small, independently deployable services. Pros: independent scaling, technology diversity, faster development. Cons: operational complexity, network latency, data consistency issues, and debugging challenges.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what are microservices? what are the pros and cons. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Microservices are small, independently deployable services.\n2. Pros: independent scaling, technology diversity, faster development.\n3. Cons: operational complexity, network latency, data consistency issues, and debugging challenges.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what you gain, what you give up, and what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Microservices are small, independently deployable services.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production platform, what are microservices? what are the pros and cons affects component boundaries, capacity, latency, consistency, or failure handling. The design should start from traffic and reliability assumptions, then choose the simplest architecture that meets them.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
