    slug: "orchestration-vs-choreography",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two for microservices.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nOrchestration uses a central conductor to coordinate services (e.g., Saga orchestrator). Choreography relies on event-driven communication without a central controller, where services react to events (e.g., using Kafka). Orchestration is simpler to monitor; choreography is more decoupled.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between orchestration and choreography and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Orchestration uses a central conductor to coordinate services (e.g., Saga orchestrator).\n2. Choreography relies on event-driven communication without a central controller, where services react to events (e.g., using Kafka).\n3. Orchestration is simpler to monitor; choreography is more decoupled.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Orchestration uses a central conductor to coordinate services (e.g., Saga orchestrator).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Orchestration uses a central conductor to coordinate services (e.g., Saga orchestrator).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
