    shortDescription: "Define and list concepts.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nKafka is a distributed event streaming platform. Core concepts: Topics (categories of messages), Partitions (ordered, immutable logs), Producers (publish), Consumers (subscribe), and Brokers (nodes).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Apache Kafka and what are its core concepts accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform.\n2. Core concepts: Topics (categories of messages), Partitions (ordered, immutable logs), Producers (publish), Consumers (subscribe), and Brokers (nodes).\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
