    question: "Explain the CAP theorem.",
    slug: "cap-theorem",
    shortDescription: "Describe Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe CAP theorem states that in a distributed system, you can only guarantee two out of three: Consistency (every read receives the most recent write), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (the system continues to operate despite network partitions). In practice, you choose between CP (strong consistency) and AP (high availability).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the CAP theorem. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The CAP theorem states that in a distributed system, you can only guarantee two out of three: Consistency (every read receives the most recent write), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (the system continues to operate despite network partitions).\n2. In practice, you choose between CP (strong consistency) and AP (high availability).\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The CAP theorem states that in a distributed system, you can only guarantee two out of three: Consistency (every read receives the most recent write), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (the system continues to operate despite network partitions).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. The CAP theorem states that in a distributed system, you can only guarantee two out of three: Consistency (every read receives the most recent write), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (the system continues to operate despite network partitions).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
