    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTCP is connection-oriented, reliable, and ensures ordered delivery. It is used for web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS). UDP is connectionless, unreliable, but faster and used for real-time applications (video streaming, DNS).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the difference between TCP and UDP. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, and ensures ordered delivery.\n2. It is used for web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS).\n3. UDP is connectionless, unreliable, but faster and used for real-time applications (video streaming, DNS).\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, and ensures ordered delivery.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, and ensures ordered delivery.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
