    shortDescription: "Explain common caching patterns.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCache-Aside: application reads from cache; if not present, it reads from DB and populates cache. Write-Through: writes go to both cache and DB. Write-Behind: writes go to cache, and writes to DB asynchronously. Also, Read-Through and Refresh-Ahead.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Cache-Aside: application reads from cache; if not present, it reads from DB and populates cache.\n2. Write-Through: writes go to both cache and DB.\n3. Write-Behind: writes go to cache, and writes to DB asynchronously.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Cache-Aside: application reads from cache; if not present, it reads from DB and populates cache.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. Cache-Aside: application reads from cache; if not present, it reads from DB and populates cache.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
