    slug: "sql-vs-nosql",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two paradigms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL databases are relational, use structured schemas, and support ACID transactions. NoSQL databases are non-relational, schema-less, and scale horizontally. NoSQL types: document (MongoDB), key-value (Redis), wide-column (Cassandra), graph (Neo4j).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between sql and nosql databases and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SQL databases are relational, use structured schemas, and support ACID transactions.\n2. NoSQL databases are non-relational, schema-less, and scale horizontally.\n3. NoSQL types: document (MongoDB), key-value (Redis), wide-column (Cassandra), graph (Neo4j).\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. SQL databases are relational, use structured schemas, and support ACID transactions.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. SQL databases are relational, use structured schemas, and support ACID transactions.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
