    shortDescription: "Explain concurrency control.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPessimistic locking assumes conflicts will happen and locks the record for the transaction. Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare; it reads without locking and checks for conflicts before committing (using version numbers).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between optimistic and pessimistic locking and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Pessimistic locking assumes conflicts will happen and locks the record for the transaction.\n2. Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare; it reads without locking and checks for conflicts before committing (using version numbers).\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Pessimistic locking assumes conflicts will happen and locks the record for the transaction.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Pessimistic locking assumes conflicts will happen and locks the record for the transaction.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
