    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEvent sourcing stores the state of a system as a sequence of events. CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) separates read and write models. They are often used together to handle complex business logic and scalability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the concept of event sourcing and CQRS. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Event sourcing stores the state of a system as a sequence of events.\n2. CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) separates read and write models.\n3. They are often used together to handle complex business logic and scalability.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Event sourcing stores the state of a system as a sequence of events.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. Event sourcing stores the state of a system as a sequence of events.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
