    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse non-blocking I/O (Netty, NIO) to handle many connections per thread. Use event-driven architecture with a message queue. Load balance connections using L4 load balancers (e.g., HAProxy). Use a service mesh for internal communication.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you design a system to handle millions of concurrent connections (like a chat server), including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use non-blocking I/O (Netty, NIO) to handle many connections per thread.\n2. Use event-driven architecture with a message queue.\n3. Load balance connections using L4 load balancers (e.g., HAProxy).\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use non-blocking I/O (Netty, NIO) to handle many connections per thread.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use non-blocking I/O (Netty, NIO) to handle many connections per thread.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
