    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA data lake stores raw, unstructured data at scale (e.g., S3, HDFS). A data warehouse stores structured, processed data optimized for analysis (e.g., Redshift, BigQuery). Lakes are for storage, warehouses for analytics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a data lake and how is it different from a data warehouse accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A data lake stores raw, unstructured data at scale (e.g., S3, HDFS).\n2. A data warehouse stores structured, processed data optimized for analysis (e.g., Redshift, BigQuery).\n3. Lakes are for storage, warehouses for analytics.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A data lake stores raw, unstructured data at scale (e.g., S3, HDFS).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A data lake stores raw, unstructured data at scale (e.g., S3, HDFS).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
