    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse a combination of logging (ELK), metrics (Prometheus, Grafana), and distributed tracing (Jaeger, Zipkin). Set up alerts based on SLIs. Use service mesh for observability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you monitor the health of a microservices ecosystem, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use a combination of logging (ELK), metrics (Prometheus, Grafana), and distributed tracing (Jaeger, Zipkin).\n2. Set up alerts based on SLIs.\n3. Use service mesh for observability.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use a combination of logging (ELK), metrics (Prometheus, Grafana), and distributed tracing (Jaeger, Zipkin).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use a combination of logging (ELK), metrics (Prometheus, Grafana), and distributed tracing (Jaeger, Zipkin).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
