    question: "What is the difference between a distributed lock and a semaphore in a distributed context?",
    slug: "distributed-lock-vs-semaphore",
    shortDescription: "Explain their use.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA distributed lock ensures exclusive access to a shared resource. A semaphore allows a limited number of concurrent accesses. Implementations use Redis, ZooKeeper, etc.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a distributed lock and a semaphore in a distributed context and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A distributed lock ensures exclusive access to a shared resource.\n2. A semaphore allows a limited number of concurrent accesses.\n3. Implementations use Redis, ZooKeeper, etc.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A distributed lock ensures exclusive access to a shared resource.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A distributed lock ensures exclusive access to a shared resource.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
