    slug: "read-through-cache",
    shortDescription: "Define the caching pattern.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIn a read-through cache, the cache is treated as the primary data source. When a request is made, the cache first checks if the data exists; if not, it loads it from the database and stores it, then returns it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a read-through cache accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. In a read-through cache, the cache is treated as the primary data source.\n2. When a request is made, the cache first checks if the data exists; if not, it loads it from the database and stores it, then returns it.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. In a read-through cache, the cache is treated as the primary data source.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. In a read-through cache, the cache is treated as the primary data source.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
