    shortDescription: "Explain the terms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFailover is the process of switching to a redundant system when the primary fails. Failback is the process of restoring the primary system and switching back to it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between failover and failback and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Failover is the process of switching to a redundant system when the primary fails.\n2. Failback is the process of restoring the primary system and switching back to it.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Failover is the process of switching to a redundant system when the primary fails.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Failover is the process of switching to a redundant system when the primary fails.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
